@node LibreClaw Remote Mode
@section LibreClaw Remote Mode

The @emph{remote} mode of @code{cmacs-libreclaw} dials @emph{out} from
cmacs to a separately-running libreclaw server at its bridge endpoint
(@code{/api/v1/bridge}, WebSocket), and tunnels cmacs's own MCP tool
surface back across the connection.  The remote server treats the
bridge as a first-class chat channel --- any room it serves can route
messages into cmacs, and the agent running on the remote server can
invoke cmacs's MCP tools as if cmacs were local to it.

This is the counterpart to @emph{embedded} mode (the default), in
which cmacs starts its own @code{LcApp} in-process via
@code{(cmacs-libreclaw-start)}.

Added in @b{libreclaw 0.21.0}, which is now the minimum version
required by @code{--with-cmacs-libreclaw}.

@subsection Why two modes?

@multitable @columnfractions .15 .25 .60
@headitem Mode @tab Where libreclaw runs @tab Use case
@item embedded @tab In the cmacs process       @tab Single-user desktop, lowest latency
@item remote   @tab A separate server          @tab Shared rooms, server-side rate limits, shared tool surface across many editors
@end multitable

@subsection Architecture

@verbatim
[ remote libreclaw server ]                [ local cmacs ]

 LcBridgeServer (libsoup3)             ⇌    LcBridgeClient
   ↓ on accept                                ↓ McpServer (cmacs's
 LcBridgeChannel  (impl LcChannel +            tools/resources/
                   LcBridge)                   prompts registered)
   ↓                                           ↓ McpMuxTransport
 LcChannelManager (existing)           ⇌    same wire frames over WSS
   ↓
 LcSession / ai-glib  ← LcBridgeToolRegistry
                        aggregates tools from
                        every connected bridge
@end verbatim

The bridge is a generic substrate, not cmacs-specific.  Any tool host
that speaks the bridge wire protocol can register as an MCP endpoint
on the remote server --- cmacs is just the first consumer.

@subsection Quick start

@subsubsection On the libreclaw server

Generate a bridge-scoped API key and install it.  In libreclaw 0.21.0
the server-side bridge is built as a library target; full YAML
auto-wiring lands in a follow-up.  Until then, a small embedding
wrapper can use @code{lc_bridge_server_new} /
@code{lc_bridge_server_start} directly.  See
@file{deps/libreclaw/docs/bridge.md} for a worked example.

The key must have @code{bridge} in its scope set:

@example
- name:       cmacs-zach
  key:        "<long random string>"
  scopes:     ["bridge"]
  rate_limit: 600/min
@end example

@subsubsection On the cmacs side

Store the bearer token via @code{auth-source} once (e.g. in
@file{~/.authinfo.gpg} keyed by the URL's host and port):

@example
machine bridge.example.com port 7077 password <bridge-token>
@end example

Then:

@example
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-remote-connect RET
    bridge.example.com RET
    ;; token auto-fetched from auth-source
@end example

The URL prompt accepts shorthand --- a bare hostname expands to
@code{ws://HOST:7077/api/v1/bridge} (the libreclaw bridge
defaults).  You can also pass @code{host:port},
@code{scheme://host}, or a full URL --- see
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-remote--normalize-url} for the rules.  In
particular, @code{wss://bridge.example.com} keeps the
scheme-default port (443) for TLS-terminating reverse proxies.

Check status:

@example
(cmacs-libreclaw-remote-connected-p) ; => t
(cmacs-libreclaw-remote-status)      ; one-line *Messages*
@end example

Rooms the remote server serves appear as @code{cmacs-libreclaw-room-mode}
org buffers with channel-id @code{"bridge"}.  Posting in a buffer
with the room id sends a @code{chat.message_out} frame; messages from
elsewhere in the room arrive as @code{chat.message_in} and surface
via the existing @code{cmacs-libreclaw--on-message} handler.

@subsubsection Local-style chat buffer

For an "always there" chat with the remote agent --- equivalent to
libreclaw's built-in stdin/stdout local channel, but flowing over
the bridge --- use:

@example
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-remote-chat
@end example

This opens (or pops to) a single shared room buffer named
@file{*libreclaw:bridge:local*}.  Compose your message in the
@code{* Compose} section and hit @kbd{C-c C-c} to send.  Replies
from the remote agent surface in the same buffer.

The buffer uses room id @code{"local"} by default; override with
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-remote-local-room-id} if you want a
different id, or set @code{cmacs-libreclaw-remote-local-room-name}
to change the buffer's display label.

For arbitrary room ids:

@example
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-remote-open-room
  some-room-id RET
  Optional display name RET
@end example

How it works end-to-end: cmacs ships @code{chat.message_out} with
the chosen room id; the server's @code{LcBridgeChannel} decodes it
and emits @code{LcChannel::message-received} as if the message came
from the bridge channel; the router routes through a session to
your AI provider; the response is sent back via
@code{lc_channel_send_message_async} on the bridge channel,
encoded as @code{chat.message_in}, and surfaces in your buffer.

@subsection Knowing what the user is looking at

The bridge registers cmacs's whole MCP tool surface as an endpoint
(@code{build_self_mcp_server} calls the same
@code{cmacs_mcp_register_all_tools} the local socket uses), so a remote
agent can read the user's editor directly.  What it cannot do is guess
@emph{which} buffer --- so every outgoing message carries a listing of
the visible ones, and the agent resolves ``here'' against that.

The tool to reach for is @code{current_view}: it returns the same
listing, freshly computed, and with @code{include_content} the buffers'
text as well.  Use it when the listing in the prompt looks stale ---
the user has been typing since they sent the message.

One caveat specific to remote mode: the agent runs on the
@emph{server's} filesystem.  Any path in the listing is meaningful only
through the tunnelled MCP tools, which resolve against the editor;
opening it with the agent's own file tools will read the wrong machine,
or nothing.

@xref{LibreClaw Usage}, for what rides the message and how to turn it
off.

@subsection Configuration

@table @code
@item cmacs-libreclaw-remote-mcp-servers
List of MCP endpoint specs to expose to the remote libreclaw.
Default @code{(cmacs)} --- the symbol @code{cmacs} wraps cmacs's own
MCP tool / resource / prompt surface as endpoint id @code{"cmacs"}.
Other spec kinds (@code{:stdio CMD}, @code{:unix-socket PATH},
@code{:ws URL}) are reserved for future versions; entries the C side
doesn't recognise are silently ignored.

@item cmacs-libreclaw-remote-display-name
Display name advertised to the remote libreclaw server.  @code{nil}
sends a generic identifier.

@item cmacs-libreclaw-remote-user-id
Stable @code{sender_id} stamped on every outbound
@code{chat.message_out} frame.  When non-nil, this value is what
the libreclaw server matches against
@code{session.command_allowed_users} and
@code{messages.command_allowed_users} for command-permission
decisions.

When @code{nil} (the default), the sender id is resolved at
connect time via @code{cmacs-libreclaw-remote--default-user-id},
which returns the @code{$USER} env var, falling back to
@code{(user-login-name)}, falling back to @code{nil}.  If the
final fallback yields @code{nil} the server uses the bridge id
(@code{bridge:<key>:<seq>}) instead --- but that changes on every
reconnect, so set this (or @code{$USER}) explicitly if you want
stable command-permission matching.

Examples: @code{"zach"}, @code{"@@zach:matrix.example.com"}.

@item cmacs-libreclaw-remote-save-conversations-dir
Directory to which remote-bridge conversations are archived as
standalone @file{.org} files.  Default @code{nil} (disabled).
Independent of the embedded-mode
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-save-conversations-dir}, so the two modes
can archive to different places.  When set, each remote room
buffer is mirrored to disk as messages arrive and again on
buffer kill.

@item cmacs-libreclaw-remote-save-conversations-name-format
File-name format for archived remote conversations.  Default
@code{"%y%m%d-%H%M%S-<agent-name>.org"}.  Passed through
@code{format-time-string} with the conversation's start time,
then the literal token @code{<agent-name>} is replaced with the
remote agent's name (the server's @code{agent.name}, queried via
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-remote-agent-name}).
@end table

@subsection Enabling bot commands on the bridge

By default @kbd{M-x cmacs-libreclaw-remote-chat} sends chat through
the bridge with cmacs's @code{$USER} as the sender id (or
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-remote-user-id} if you override it).  That
sender id is what libreclaw's permission system matches against,
so to unlock the session/message-management bot commands
(@code{!close_session}, @code{!swap_model}, @code{!stop},
@code{!sessions}, @code{!status}, @code{!stats}, @code{!debug},
@code{!upgrade}, @code{!restart}, @code{!messages},
@code{!rm_message}), add your user to libreclaw's allowed-users
lists.

On the libreclaw server, in @file{config.yaml}:

@example
session:
  command_allowed_users:
    - "zach"             # must match the sender_id cmacs sends

messages:
  command_allowed_users:
    - "zach"
@end example

On cmacs, the simplest setup is to do nothing --- the default value
of @code{cmacs-libreclaw-remote-user-id} resolves to @code{$USER}.
If your shell user name matches the entry in
@code{command_allowed_users} you're done.

For a non-shell identity (e.g. the matrix-style fully qualified
user id), set it explicitly and reconnect:

@example
(setq cmacs-libreclaw-remote-user-id "@@zach:matrix.example.com")
@end example

After @code{!help} in a bridge room, you should see the full
session and message command blocks if your user is allowed.

@subsection Server-side knobs that affect bridge traffic

These are libreclaw config options (not cmacs config); listed here
because they're load-bearing for the remote-mode user experience.

@subsubsection @code{channels.bridge.model} --- pin a model for bridge channels

Added in libreclaw 0.21.0.  When set, every bridge channel uses the
specified model and smart routing is suppressed for those turns:

@example
channels:
  bridge:
    model: "opus"
    # model: "claude-opus-tmux"   # named provider from providers:
@end example

A per-message @code{$OVERRIDE(model=<x>)} directive in the body
still wins (and likewise skips routing), so you can carve out
one-off escalations without changing the default.

Resolution order for a bridge message:

@enumerate
@item global @code{ai.model} / @code{ai.provider}
@item @code{channels.bridge.model} --- suppresses smart routing
@item @code{routing.*} --- only consulted if step 2 is unset
@item @code{$OVERRIDE(model=...)} in the body --- always wins
@end enumerate

@subsubsection @code{libreclaw-bridge-mcp} shim --- server-side claude-code

The libreclaw repo ships a small stdio MCP binary
(@code{libreclaw-bridge-mcp}) that lets a claude-code running on the
libreclaw server box invoke cmacs MCP tools through the bridge,
without needing a direct socket connection to cmacs.

Flow:
@code{claude-code (stdio) -> libreclaw-bridge-mcp ->
/api/v1/bridge/tools/call (REST) -> bridge -> cmacs}
(and the response retraces the path).

Setup is documented in @file{deps/libreclaw/docs/bridge.md}
(``libreclaw-bridge-mcp shim'').  Briefly: install the binary on the
server, issue an API key with scopes @code{bridge.read} +
@code{bridge.invoke}, add an entry to claude-code's
@file{.mcp.json} that runs @code{libreclaw-bridge-mcp} with
@code{--api-url} and @code{--token-env}, and restart claude-code.
Tools are enumerated at shim startup.

@subsection Wire protocol (v1)

Every frame is one WebSocket text message containing a JSON envelope:

@example
@{ "v": 1, "kind": "<kind>", "id": "<corr-id?>", "payload": @{ ... @} @}
@end example

Three planes:

@multitable @columnfractions .15 .85
@headitem Plane @tab Kinds
@item @code{control.} @tab hello, welcome, ping, pong, bye, error
@item @code{chat.}    @tab message_in, message_out, typing, room.add, room.remove
@item @code{mcp.}     @tab request, response, notify, endpoints_changed
@end multitable

MCP frames are addressed to a named endpoint so a single bridge
connection can multiplex multiple MCP sub-servers:

@example
@{ "v":1, "kind": "mcp.request",
  "payload": @{ "endpoint": "cmacs",
               "frame":    @{ "jsonrpc": "2.0",
                             "id":      "42",
                             "method":  "tools/call",
                             "params":  @{ "name":"cmacs-eval",
                                          "arguments": @{ "form":"(+ 1 2)" @} @} @} @} @}
@end example

Correlation between request and response uses the @b{inner JSON-RPC
@code{id}} (not the envelope-level @code{id}), so the McpServer on
the remote side can round-trip it unchanged.

Authentication: bearer token on the WSS upgrade @code{Authorization}
header, validated against @code{LcApiKey} entries with scope
@code{bridge}.  Reuses the per-key rate limit + IP allowlist that the
REST API already enforces.

@subsection Exposing additional MCP servers

The default @code{cmacs-libreclaw-remote-mcp-servers} is
@code{(cmacs)}.  Future spec kinds (@code{:stdio CMD},
@code{:unix-socket PATH}, @code{:ws URL}) are reserved; unknown
entries are silently ignored in 0.21.0.

@subsection Troubleshooting

@table @code
@item cmacs-libreclaw-error: remote bridge not connected
Connect didn't reach the server --- check URL, firewall, that the
libreclaw bridge listener is up.

@item Connect succeeds but rooms never appear
The remote server's bridge listener accepted the connection but the
agent isn't routing rooms to it yet.

@item MCP tool calls hang on the server side
The matching tool isn't registered on cmacs's MCP server; check
@code{(cmacs-mcp-server-p)} is non-nil.
@end table

See also @ref{LibreClaw API Reference} for the full DEFUN reference
and @file{deps/libreclaw/docs/bridge.md} for the wire-level codec
specification.
